<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oskar Kokoschka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1886">1886</a>–<a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kokoschka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oskar Kokoschka was an Austrian expressionist painter, printmaker and writer, born in 1886. He was part of the cultural flowering of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> at the turn of the twentieth century. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> count this Viennese culture as the empire's most significant contribution to European civilisation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oskar-kokoschka.jpg" alt="Oskar Kokoschka" width="223" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Erling Mandelmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oskar_Kokoschka_(1963)_by_Erling_Mandelmann.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Oskar Kokoschka was born on 1 March 1886 in Pöchlarn, the second child of the goldsmith Gustav Josef Kokoschka and Maria Romana Kokoschka. His father's unstable finances meant constant moves to smaller flats, and Kokoschka drew close to his mother. At a Realschule he excelled only in art, and a teacher who admired his drawings suggested a career in the fine arts. Against his father's will he entered the Kunstgewerbeschule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, a progressive school dominated by teachers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna-secession">Vienna Secession</a>, on a scholarship. He studied there from 1904 to 1909 and was influenced by Carl Otto Czeschka.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1908 he showed the lithograph book Die träumenden Knaben and a tapestry at the first Vienna Kunstschau. Their sexualised fantasies provoked a backlash from conservative officials. He was expelled from the Kunstgewerbeschule. The architect Adolf Loos befriended him and introduced him to the Viennese avant-garde, whose members he painted in portraits between 1909 and 1914; Loos often ordered these and bought them if the sitter did not, which left Kokoschka free. The portraits use harsh colour, scratched lines and visible brushwork, and give prominence to hands. In 1910 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where Herwarth Walden employed him as an illustrator for the magazine Der Sturm and Paul Cassirer took him up.</p><p>His affair with Alma Mahler, begun in 1912, ended two years later, and The Bride of the Wind of 1913 expresses it. He volunteered as a cavalryman in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and was seriously wounded in 1915. He taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> from 1911 to 1913 and at the Dresden Kunstakademie from 1919 to 1923. The Nazis deemed him degenerate, and he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1934, took Czechoslovak citizenship in 1935 and fled to the United Kingdom in 1938. There he painted political allegories, among them The Crab and The Red Egg, which refers to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> of 1938.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In 1912 he delivered the essay On the Nature of Visions, which set out a relation between inner vision and optical sight; art historians take it, with Kandinsky's ideas on spirituality in art, as a basis for Viennese Expressionism. His short play Murderer, the Hope of Women (1909) is often called the first Expressionist drama, and Paul Hindemith set it as an opera. His autobiography, A Sea Ringed with Visions, appeared in 1962. In teaching he drew on the educator Jan Amos Comenius and held that pupils reason best through the five senses.</p><p>He became a British subject in 1947, settled at Villeneuve in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in 1953 and regained Austrian citizenship only in 1978. The Tate Gallery held a retrospective in 1962, and he won the Erasmus Prize in 1960. He died at Montreux on 22 February 1980, aged 93.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oskar Kokoschka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…intellectual capitals of the Western world, producing the foundations of psychoanalysis (Freud), political Zionism (Herzl), the art of the Secession and Expressionism (Klimt, Schiele, <b>Kokoschka</b>), the Second Viennese School of music (Schoenberg, Berg, Webern), and the Austrian school of economics (Menger), while nurturing thinkers such as Ludwig Wittgenstein.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Cubism arose in the studios of Henri Matisse, Pablo Picasso and Georges Braque between 1900 and 1910; in 1907 Picasso painted Les Demoiselles d’Avignon and <b>Oskar Kokoschka</b> wrote the first Expressionist play, and Arnold Schoenberg’s Second String Quartet of 1908 was his first composition without a tonal centre.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pöchlarn, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Montreux, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.2,15.2&amp;year=1947" aria-label="Pöchlarn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Oskar Kokoschka"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4166 5908h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4166 5908h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.2,15.2&amp;year=1947">What was here in 1947? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
